From 95fec98b62ad0ad84fa26069ed4284b1cdf7c402 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LukeOkk <85941941+LukeOkk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 02:10:51 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 5/9] runtime, dex2oat: boot images and oat files in the heap
 reference window (SteamARM, HYPOTHESIS)

HYPOTHESIS, UNTESTED: not compiled, not run.

The boot image keeps its layout: its 32-bit references and its header's
32-bit address fields are the low halves of addresses, the same for any
heap reference base. The runtime puts it in the window:

- the reservation is at kHeapReferenceBase + ART_BASE_ADDRESS (+ the ASLR
  delta);
- ImageHeader's address getters, image roots and image methods add the
  base back;
- relocation (SimpleRelocateVisitor, SplitRangeRelocateVisitor,
  MaybeRelocateSpaces, the app image boot range) already ran in 32-bit
  arithmetic through reinterpret_cast32; it keeps doing so on the low
  halves and ORs the base into every relocated pointer, heap reference and
  native pointer alike (ArtMethod, ArtField, dex cache arrays, code).

So an upstream boot image built for the low 4 GiB relocates into the window
as it is. Its compiled code does not decompress references, so it must not
run: an oat file records the base its code was generated for
("heap-reference-base", written by dex2oat for arm64 when non-zero; absent
means 0), and a mismatch rejects the boot image's oat file and makes an
app's oat file kOatCannotOpen, unless the runtime is interpret-only
(-Xint). -Xint gives every non-native boot image method the interpreter
bridge (ClassLinker::InitFromBootImage); native methods keep their compiled
JNI stubs, which only store the low halves of references into the handle
scope and so work with any base (see the SteamARM study).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01L6ANQQienC2qRCeZBzUfuS
